>normally result in significant variety really ends up being only a coin
>flip... gray or silver?)

I think the problem can be summarised as:

 - You can get all the "elemental" resistances as intrinsics, and those
   resistances reduce the damage those elemental attacks do to *zero*.

 - Reflection makes dragons laughable when they ought to be scary.

 - There are attacks that no character can ever afford to be normally
   affected by.
 
 - DSM has one flaw (MC 0), which is trivially addressable by your
   choice of cloak.

A few thoughts...

 - Leaky reflection. Reflection should let 10% of the beam strength
   through and send 90% of it back. Disintegration or death ray leakage
   would do damage equal to 10% of your max HP if you don't have the
   corresponding resistance, and anyone who ate the reflected beam would
   only lose 90% of theirs instead of being whacked outright.

 - Leaky fire/cold/elec/acid resistance - again, they should let through
   a fraction of the HP damage (*always* rounding up); only polymorphing
   into a resistant monster, or wearing the appropriate DSM, should zero
   out the damage.

 - Replace deadly poison's "Surprise! You're DEAD!" with something a bit
   less... brutal. Have it do bonus damage equal to... 25+1d50 % of your
   max HP, or something.

 - Death rays, touch of death, etc. should be partially resistable even
   without binary magic resistance - possibly some interaction of
   Constitution and armour enchantment? They should still *hurt* if you
   don't have MR, but they shouldn't be an autokill against a powerful
   character.

 - David has the right of it, I think, where melee damage from
   high-level enemies is concerned - high-level melee monsters should do
   a lot of damage, and should do it in relatively concentrated lumps.
   A jabberwock can nominally do 80 points of damage in one round; in
   practice it'll be doing well to do 20 against a late-game character.
